:[],"digest":"761567c1ad80c72ac0083db43567c708c914a9200949fe6a1daea74173f4697a"},"namespace_version":1,"reason":null,"unit_id":"agent:T5c","usage":{"input_tokens":22233,"output_tokens":840,"requests":3}},{"kind":"success","lineage":{"dependencies":[],"digest":"52bf8e958dec601ea62a735673cf10521192c6df6bbfb0b08933e0fecd7f9f3b"},"namespace_version":1,"reason":null,"unit_id":"agent:E4","usage":{"input_tokens":133615,"output_tokens":3131,"requests":6}},{"kind":"success","lineage":{"dependencies":[],"digest":"2f1cf63136900a1f9c0b316b88cf6b0b7930f696187605d8c59db232446fd974"},"namespace_version":1,"reason":null,"unit_id":"agent:G6","usage":{"input_tokens":76713,"output_tokens":4825,"requests":6}},{"kind":"success","lineage":{"dependencies":[],"digest":"a7960440d885d5b31ca803506fb36a01b563c1fa53f07edbce3538b5966d0348"},"namespace_version":1,"reason":null,"unit_id":"agent:project.symbol-reference-completeness","usage":{"input_tokens":12564,"output_tokens":546,"requests":2}},{"kind":"success","lineage":{"dependencies":[],"digest":"a6e260c9558e0ca9eab920e273b2480ff60d7d6e11e5f641377f472035b826c0"},"namespace_version":1,"reason":null,"unit_id":"single:COH,E1,E2,E6,F1,T2,T4,T5b,T5e,T9","usage":{"input_tokens":10146,"output_tokens":5423,"requests":1}},{"kind":"success","lineage":{"dependencies":[],"digest":"d1b1bf39fb60fbd5260b1d51afe5906b598c6cb48865742fea105d83494ca349"},"namespace_version":1,"reason":null,"unit_id":"agent:T3","usage":{"input_tokens":48045,"output_tokens":2389,"requests":4}}],"version":1},"findings":[{"block_threshold":0.6,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Pin the cause of each expected OpcertKeyError with `match=`: the fail-closed test matches the ssh-keygen malformed-key message (\"malformed\"), and the POSIX branch of the group-readable test matches \"0600 or stricter\".","cohort":["G6"],"criteria":["G6"],"decision":"advisory","decision_margin":-0.1875,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Plan Cause: 'the test passed on Windows only by accident: compose always failed ... and that raised the expected OpcertKeyError'","Plan Approach: 'compose_startup_opcert_binding must raise OpcertKeyError (from ssh-keygen -y validation of the copy)'. The cause is stated, but no assertion pins it.","keyprov.py:131-135 raises '... must be 0600 or stricter'; keyprov.py:168-172 raises 'the op-cert key is malformed, encrypted ...'; keyprov.py:195-216 raises OpcertKeyError for whoami/icacls failures.","Repo convention: tests/unit/test_windows_mode_bit_skip.py:55 uses pytest.raises(keyprov.OpcertKeyError, match=\"0600 or stricter\")"],"finding":"Both new OpcertKeyError assertions are specified only by exception type. compose_signer raises OpcertKeyError for many unrelated reasons: whoami/icacls failure on Windows, ssh-keygen missing, the source mode check, a non-Ed25519 key. So the rewritten fail-closed test can again pass by accident, which is the failure mode this plan is fixing. For example, if the test's 'bad content' file is written with the default umask (0644) and not explicitly chmod'ed to 0600, then on POSIX it is rejected by the permission check, not by ssh-keygen. On Windows, an icacls/whoami failure would also satisfy the assertion. In the same way, the POSIX branch of the group-readable test would accept any OpcertKeyError, not specifically the 0600 rejection.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f5d14c9f423ec6895","impact":0.55,"is_primary":null,"location":"Approach — first and second bullets (tests/unit/test_mcp_opcert_signing_goose.py)","norm_id":"n1679b022d3117d0c","priority":0.4125,"reason":"default-advisory","recalled":false,"scenarios":["The implementer writes the non-OpenSSH content with write_text and forgets chmod(0o600). On macOS/Linux the test passes through the permission check, and the ssh-keygen path is never exercised.","A future Windows ACL regression makes _restrict_dir_to_owner fail. The fail-closed test keeps passing and hides that compose is broken again, exactly like the pre-3462 situation."],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"In test_compose_startup_binding_fails_closed_on_bad_key, write the non-OpenSSH content, call Path(bad).chmod(0o600) explicitly, and assert `with pytest.raises(OpcertKeyError, match=\"malformed\")`. In test_compose_startup_binding_group_readable_key_follows_the_platform_rule, use `pytest.raises(OpcertKeyError, match=\"0600 or stricter\")` in the _POSIX_MODE_BITS branch.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.75,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"yes","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"yes","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"yes","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"yes"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"low","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"underspecified_certification","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"medium","prod_impact":"none","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"silent","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"none"}}},{"block_threshold":null,"blocking_enabled":null,"checklist_item":"","cohort":null,"criteria":["P6"],"decision":"advisory","decision_margin":null,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["1 criterion line(s) bundle multiple deliverables with 'and' (split so each is independently verifiable)."],"finding":"Acceptance-criteria / definition-of-done quality issues.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f607dd457c67f9209","impact":0.4,"is_primary":null,"location":"","norm_id":"ned06ebc801a522e3","priority":0.4,"reason":null,"recalled":false,"scenarios":[],"severity":"minor","suggested_fix":"Split compound criteria, replace subjective terms with observable outcomes, and state how each is verified.","tier":"DET","validity":1.0,"verification":null},{"block_threshold":0.95,"blocking_enabled":false,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Give a concrete, reproducible command for the forced-Windows run, or commit an extra case that monkeypatches `keyprov._POSIX_MODE_BITS=False` and `_restrict_dir_to_owner` to a no-op, so the compose-succeeds-then-cleanup branch runs in `make verify` on every platform.","cohort":["COH","E1","E2","E6","F1","T2","T4","T5b","T5e","T9"],"criteria":["E6"],"decision":"advisory","decision_margin":-0.73,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Testing: 'Both tests pass on macOS (`make verify`), and on macOS with the Windows branch forced as in Reproduction.'","Reproduction: 'On macOS with the Windows branch forced (`keyprov._POSIX_MODE_BITS = False`, `_restrict_dir_to_owner` a no-op)' gives no committed mechanism or command."],"finding":"The plan verifies the Windows branch on macOS by 'forcing' it as in Reproduction. That is a manual, uncommitted patch, and no command for it is given. On macOS and Linux CI the compose-succeeds branch of the new test never executes. The only check on that branch is the post-merge Windows sweep, so a defect in it would be found only after merge.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"fd5f913a269318bf1","impact":0.44,"is_primary":null,"location":"Testing, bullet 1 / Reproduction","norm_id":"nfd300b3dc354e53d","priority":0.22,"reason":"default-advisory","recalled":false,"scenarios":["The Windows branch has a bug, such as a wrong cleanup() call or a wrong assertion. It passes `make verify` on macOS, and the bug surfaces only in the next Windows sweep on main."],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"Add a monkeypatch-based case, e.g. a parametrized variant or a sibling test, that sets `keyprov._POSIX_MODE_BITS` to False and replaces `keyprov._restrict_dir_to_owner` with a no-op. It then asserts that compose succeeds with the 0644 key and calls `cleanup()`. Keep the unpatched platform-native test as well, so the real Windows path is still covered by the Windows sweep.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.5,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"yes","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"yes","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"no","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"no"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"underspecified_oracle","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"low","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"low","prod_impact":"none","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"self_revealing","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"none"}}},{"block_threshold":0.95,"blocking_enabled":false,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Either drop the new 0644 binding-level test because test_windows_mode_bit_skip.py and test_rp04_s6_opcert_startup.py:192 already pin the rule, or parametrize it over `_POSIX_MODE_BITS` in {True, False} via monkeypatch so both branches run on every platform, matching the repo convention.","cohort":["A1"],"criteria":["A1"],"decision":"advisory","decision_margin":-0.95,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Plan: \"It asserts `OpcertKeyError` where `keyprov._POSIX_MODE_BITS` is true. Where it is false (Windows), it asserts that compose succeeds\"","tests/unit/test_windows_mode_bit_skip.py:7 — \"Each module's ``_POSIX_MODE_BITS`` constant selects the branch, so both branches run on every platform.\" Lines 53-63 already pin the 0644 reject branch (True) and the accept branch (False) via monkeypatch.setattr.","tests/unit/test_rp04_s6_opcert_startup.py:192-204 `test_compose_rejects_too_permissive_key_path` already pins the POSIX rejection at the compose_signer level, using monkeypatch.setattr(keyprov, \"_POSIX_MODE_BITS\", True) and chmod(0o644).","Plan Testing relies on a manual one-off run (\"on macOS with the Windows branch forced as in Reproduction\") to exercise the Windows branch off-Windows. Nothing in the committed test makes CI do this."],"finding":"The new `test_compose_startup_binding_group_readable_key_follows_the_platform_rule` covers ground that existing tests already pin on every platform. It also departs from the repo's both-branches convention: it asserts whichever branch the live `keyprov._POSIX_MODE_BITS` selects, so a normal macOS/Linux run only exercises the raise branch, and a Windows run only exercises the compose-succeeds branch. Existing tests already cover both rules on every platform by monkeypatching the switch.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f1a5b0dd6047a3814","impact":0.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Approach — second bullet (new test_compose_startup_binding_group_readable_key_follows_the_platform_rule); Testing — \"on macOS with the Windows branch forced\"","norm_id":"nd8a243c85961c162","priority":0.0,"reason":"default-advisory","recalled":false,"scenarios":["On macOS/Linux CI the compose-succeeds-and-cleanup() branch never runs, so a regression there only shows up in the Windows sweep.","On Windows CI the raise branch never runs, so a binding-level regression in POSIX rejection only shows up in the POSIX sweeps."],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"Write the new test as `@pytest.mark.parametrize(\"mode_bits\", [True, False])` with `monkeypatch.setattr(keyprov, \"_POSIX_MODE_BITS\", mode_bits)`. Assert `pytest.raises(OpcertKeyError, match=\"0600 or stricter\")` when True; when False, assert compose succeeds and call `cleanup()`. This mirrors tests/unit/test_windows_mode_bit_skip.py:66-70, and the manual \"forced Windows branch\" step in Testing becomes part of the committed test.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.875,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"na","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"no","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"yes","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"yes","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"yes"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"low","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"medium","prod_impact":"none","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"self_revealing","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"none"}}},{"block_threshold":0.6,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Make AC2 require that both `test_compose_startup_binding_fails_closed_on_bad_key` and `test_compose_startup_binding_group_readable_key_follows_the_platform_rule` are reported PASSED (not skipped) in a named post-merge `sweep (windows)` run, and record the run id on the ticket.","cohort":["COH","E1","E2","E6","F1","T2","T4","T5b","T5e","T9"],"criteria":["E6","E1","F1"],"decision":"dropped","decision_margin":-0.2562,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Testing: 'a `sweep (windows)` run on main has no failures in `test_mcp_opcert_signing_goose.py`, and both tests ran (not skipped).'","AC2: 'A `sweep (windows)` run on main after the change has no failures in `test_mcp_opcert_signing_goose.py`.'","Approach: 'the test is neither skipped nor deselected, and every case runs on every platform.'"],"finding":"AC2 only requires 'no failures in test_mcp_opcert_signing_goose.py'. The Testing section also requires that 'both tests ran (not skipped)', and AC2 drops that. A Windows run where either test is skipped, deselected or not collected would still satisfy AC2.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"fb759d887425088ee","impact":0.55,"is_primary":null,"location":"Acceptance Criteria, AC2 / Testing section","norm_id":"n866a9e408565f119","priority":0.3438,"reason":"veto:absence-refuted","recalled":false,"scenarios":["A future skipif or collection error makes the Windows run report 0 failures for the file, and AC2 is ticked even though nothing ran."],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"Change AC2 to: 'A post-merge `sweep (windows)` run on main (run id recorded on the ticket) reports both `test_compose_startup_binding_fails_closed_on_bad_key` and `test_compose_startup_binding_group_readable_key_follows_the_platform_rule` as PASSED (not skipped), with no failures in `test_mcp_opcert_signing_goose.py`.'","tier":"LLM","validity":0.625,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"no","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"yes","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"no","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"yes"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"low","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"underspecified_certification","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"low","prod_impact":"none","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"silent","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"none"}}},{"block_threshold":0.95,"blocking_enabled":false,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Pin the platform rule independently of the product constant, e.g. assert `keyprov._POSIX_MODE_BITS == (os.name != 'nt')`, or derive the expected outcome from `os.name` / `sys.platform` instead of from the constant under test.","cohort":["COH","E1","E2","E6","F1","T2","T4","T5b","T5e","T9"],"criteria":["E6"],"decision":"dropped","decision_margin":-0.95,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["'It asserts `OpcertKeyError` where `keyprov._POSIX_MODE_BITS` is true. Where it is false (Windows), it asserts that compose succeeds ... which records the nymph decision as tested behaviour.'","'Portable: no platform-specific API in the test.'"],"finding":"The new test reads `keyprov._POSIX_MODE_BITS`, the constant under test, to decide what to expect. If that constant regressed on some platform, the test would silently switch to the other expectation instead of failing. The claim that the test 'records the nymph decision as tested behaviour' is therefore circular: it pins whatever the constant says, not the decision itself.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f60799a92b6135ec5","impact":0.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Approach, bullet 2 (new test_compose_startup_binding_group_readable_key_follows_the_platform_rule)","norm_id":"nd8fded3cfb27898d","priority":0.0,"reason":"low-validity","recalled":false,"scenarios":["A refactor sets `_POSIX_MODE_BITS` True on Windows, or False on Linux. The test follows the new value and stays green, so the platform rule is no longer pinned."],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"","tier":"LLM","validity":0.375,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"na","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"no","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"no","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"no","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"no"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"low","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"low","prod_impact":"none","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"silent","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"none"}}},{"block_threshold":0.95,"blocking_enabled":false,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Pin the platform rule independently of `keyprov._POSIX_MODE_BITS` in the group-readable test, e.g. also assert `keyprov._POSIX_MODE_BITS == (os.name != \"nt\")`, so a mis-set flag fails the test instead of switching its expectation.","cohort":["E3","E5","F4","G5","T6","necessity","project.failure-disposition-contract","project.net-mechanism","project.portability"],"criteria":["E5"],"decision":"dropped","decision_margin":-0.95,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["\"It asserts `OpcertKeyError` where `keyprov._POSIX_MODE_BITS` is true. Where it is false (Windows), it asserts that compose succeeds\"","\"a binding-level test pins the group-readable-key rule per platform (raise with POSIX mode bits, compose without)\""],"finding":"The new `test_compose_startup_binding_group_readable_key_follows_the_platform_rule` reads `keyprov._POSIX_MODE_BITS` to choose its expected outcome. That flag is the product state encoding the rule being pinned. If the flag were wrongly False on Linux/macOS, the test would assert that compose succeeds with a 0644 key and pass, silently dropping the POSIX 0600 fail-closed check it is meant to guard. The oracle is self-referential for the platform dimension.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"fb121abc4825479d8","impact":0.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Approach — second bullet (new group-readable test); Acceptance Criteria line 1","norm_id":"nbc1ca8f6c4e0a07f","priority":0.0,"reason":"low-validity","recalled":false,"scenarios":["A regression sets `_POSIX_MODE_BITS` to False on Linux (e.g. a bad platform-detection change). The new test takes the 'compose succeeds' branch and stays green, so the 0644 source-key rejection is lost with no failing test."],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"In the new test, add an explicit expectation derived from the standard library (e.g. `expected_posix = os.name != \"nt\"`; `assert keyprov._POSIX_MODE_BITS is expected_posix`). Then branch on `expected_posix`. `os.name` is portable, so this keeps the plan's 'no platform-specific API' constraint.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.375,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"na","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"na","claims_absence":"no","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"no","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"no","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"no"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"low","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"low","prod_impact":"none","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"silent","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"none"}}}],"impact_model_version":"plan-v5","material_fingerprint":"c0bfc5c46e173da2","material_parts":{"children":["647ea2107ca5072f",0],"description":["e6b6c8d93c67f642",3032],"description_boxes":["6c179f21e6f62b62",0],"file_impact":["f6fda31480f4947b",1],"ticket_id":["f591dc8f11f67766",19]},"metrics":{"cache_read_tokens":255244,"cache_write_tokens":168285,"claim_path":"no-llm/no-network (structural; the fast claim check is a local HMAC verify)","det_ms":12534.4,"input_tokens":445927,"llm_calls":28,"llm_ms":96797.2,"output_tokens":26815,"precheck":{"cited_anchor_warning":false},"total_ms":155003.6,"verify_requests":4},"model":"anthropic:claude-sonnet-5-5","provider_pro